-- title:
--   Relative group laws commute on K-points of elliptic models
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $V$ be a projective Weierstrass curve over $R$ whose associated affine Weierstrass curve is elliptic. Write $\pi_V =$ `projModelStrCR V` for the structure morphism $\operatorname{Proj}$ of the graded quotient $R[X_0,X_1,X_2]/(V.\mathrm{polynomial})$ (the grading induced on the quotient by the homogeneous ideal generated by the Weierstrass cubic) to $\operatorname{Spec} R$, given by the canonical map to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the degree-zero part followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R \to (\text{degree } 0)$. Assume the base-change hypothesis `hbc`: for every field $K$ in the ambient universe carrying an $R$-algebra structure, the pullback of $\pi_V$ along $\operatorname{Spec}(R \to K)$ is isomorphic, as a scheme, to `projModelCR (V.baseChange K)`. Let $G$ be a relative group law on $\pi_V$, that is, a family of multiplication, unit and inversion operations on the sets of sections $\{\varphi : T \to \operatorname{Proj} \mid \varphi \circ \pi_V = t\}$, for all $R$-schemes $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$, satisfying associativity, the two unit laws, left inversion, and naturality of multiplication under change of the test scheme. Then for every field $K$ with an $R$-algebra structure, and for all sections $P, Q$ of $\pi_V$ over $t_K = \operatorname{Spec}(R \to K)$, one has $G.\mathrm{mul}\,t_K\,P\,Q = G.\mathrm{mul}\,t_K\,Q\,P$. Thus commutativity is asserted here only for the fibres over $R$-fields, not for arbitrary test schemes.
--
--   Classically, any group law on a proper, geometrically integral scheme over a field is commutative, by the rigidity argument for abelian varieties; the present statement is the instance of that fact for the projective Weierstrass model of an elliptic curve, obtained fibrewise over each $R$-field $K$ rather than over an arbitrary base scheme. It is the fibrewise input to the general-base commutativity statements `isCommutative_of_isElliptic_of_baseChangeIso` and its variant over a domain, and thence to the construction of a commutative relative group law with unit the zero section.
